B.ahL=new A.fQ("Healthy Eating During Pregnancy","Nutrition",5,'Nutrition during pregnancy is foundational to both maternal and fetal health. The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG) recommends an additional 300\u2013500 calories per day during pregnancy, with an emphasis on nutrient-dense foods such as leafy greens, lean proteins, whole grains, and dairy or fortified alternatives. Key nutrients include folate (400\u2013800 mcg daily to prevent neural tube defects), iron (27 mg/day), calcium (1,000 mg/day), and omega-3 fatty acids for fetal brain development.\n\nHydration is equally critical\u2014pregnant women should aim for at least 8\u201310 glasses of water daily to support amniotic fluid levels and prevent constipation, a common complaint in pregnancy. Eating smaller, more frequent meals can help manage blood sugar levels and reduce nausea, especially in the first trimester. Foods to avoid include raw or undercooked meat and eggs, high-mercury fish (shark, swordfish, king mackerel), unpasteurized cheeses, and deli meats unless heated until steaming.\n\nA well-rounded prenatal diet also supports healthy weight gain. For women with a normal pre-pregnancy BMI, the Institute of Medicine recommends gaining 25\u201335 pounds total. Rather than "eating for two," focus on the quality of your food choices. Working with a registered dietitian can help tailor a meal plan to your specific needs, especially if you have dietary restrictions or conditions like gestational diabetes.',null)
B.ahJ=new A.fQ("Managing Morning Sickness Naturally","Pregnancy",4,"Morning sickness\u2014nausea and vomiting in pregnancy\u2014affects up to 80% of pregnant women, most commonly during the first trimester. Despite its name, it can occur at any time of day. The exact cause is not fully understood, but rising levels of hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) and estrogen are believed to play a significant role. For most women, symptoms peak around weeks 8\u201310 and subside by week 14\u201316.\n\nEvidence-based natural remedies include ginger, which has been shown in multiple randomized controlled trials to significantly reduce nausea severity. You can consume it as ginger tea, ginger chews, or ginger ale made with real ginger. Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) at doses of 10\u201325 mg three times daily is another first-line recommendation supported by ACOG. Eating small, bland, carbohydrate-rich snacks before getting out of bed and avoiding strong smells or fatty, spicy foods can also provide relief.\n\nAcupressure applied to the P6 (Neiguan) point on the inner wrist\u2014using wristbands or manual pressure\u2014has shown modest benefits in clinical studies. Staying well-hydrated, resting when possible, and getting fresh air can also ease symptoms. If nausea is severe or you are unable to keep food or fluids down, speak with your healthcare provider, as hyperemesis gravidarum may require medical treatment.",null)
B.ahO=new A.fQ("Your Second Trimester: What to Expect","Pregnancy",6,'The second trimester (weeks 13\u201326) is often referred to as the "honeymoon phase" of pregnancy. For many women, the nausea and fatigue of the first trimester begin to ease, energy levels improve, and the risk of miscarriage decreases significantly. This is also when the baby\'s movements\u2014called quickening\u2014are first felt, typically between weeks 18 and 22 for first-time mothers, and slightly earlier in subsequent pregnancies.\n\nKey developments during this period include the fetus growing from about 3 inches to nearly 14 inches in length. The baby develops fingerprints, begins to hear sounds, and the sex organs become distinguishable on ultrasound. Routine prenatal care includes the anatomy ultrasound (typically around weeks 18\u201320) and optional genetic screening tests such as the quad screen or cell-free DNA (cfDNA) testing. Iron-deficiency anemia is common and your provider may check your hemoglobin levels.\n\nPhysical changes include a visibly growing belly, possible round ligament pain (sharp twinges in the lower abdomen), skin changes like linea nigra and melasma, and increased appetite. Dental health is important\u2014pregnancy gingivitis is common due to hormonal changes, and the CDC recommends routine dental care during pregnancy. Staying active with low-impact exercise like walking, swimming, or prenatal yoga supports cardiovascular health, mood, and prepares your body for labor.',null)
B.ahM=new A.fQ("Postpartum Recovery: The Fourth Trimester","Postpartum",8,"The fourth trimester refers to the 12 weeks following childbirth\u2014a period of profound physical and emotional adjustment for new mothers. Despite being one of the most demanding periods in a woman's life, it has historically received far less medical attention than the preceding trimesters. ACOG now recommends ongoing postpartum care as a continuum rather than a single six-week check-up, with contact within the first three weeks for all women.\n\nPhysical recovery varies depending on the type of delivery. After a vaginal birth, perineal soreness and swelling are common, and healing typically occurs within 4\u20136 weeks. C-section recovery takes longer\u2014the incision site requires care, and lifting restrictions apply for several weeks. Uterine cramping (afterpains) as the uterus contracts back to its pre-pregnancy size is normal, particularly during breastfeeding. Lochia\u2014postpartum vaginal discharge\u2014can last 4\u20136 weeks and transitions from red to pink to yellow-white.\n\nEmotional changes are just as significant. The \"baby blues\"\u2014mood swings, tearfulness, and anxiety\u2014affect up to 80% of new mothers in the first two weeks postpartum and are considered normal. However, postpartum depression (PPD), which affects approximately 1 in 7 women, involves persistent sadness, inability to bond with the baby, or thoughts of self-harm and requires professional treatment. Sleep deprivation compounding these challenges is real\u2014prioritizing rest by sleeping when the baby sleeps and accepting help from your support network is not optional; it's medical advice.",null)
B.ahI=new A.fQ("Mental Health During Pregnancy","Mental Health",5,"Perinatal mental health\u2014encompassing the period from conception through the first year postpartum\u2014is a critical but often overlooked dimension of maternal care. Anxiety and depression during pregnancy are more common than most people realize: approximately 15\u201320% of pregnant women experience clinically significant anxiety, and 10\u201315% experience depression. Hormonal fluctuations, sleep disruption, life stressors, and prior mental health history all contribute to risk.\n\nCommon symptoms of antenatal depression include persistent low mood, loss of interest in activities, difficulty concentrating, changes in sleep and appetite beyond what pregnancy typically causes, and feelings of worthlessness or hopelessness. Anxiety during pregnancy may manifest as excessive worry about the baby's health, fear of childbirth (tokophobia), intrusive thoughts, or panic attacks. Screening using validated tools such as the Edinburgh Perinatal Depression Scale (EPDS) is recommended at both prenatal and postpartum visits.\n\nTreatment options are effective and should not be delayed out of stigma or concern about medications. Cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) has strong evidence for both depression and anxiety in pregnancy and carries no fetal risk. Mindfulness-based interventions have also shown benefit. When medication is necessary, several antidepressants (particularly SSRIs like sertraline) have been used safely in pregnancy, and the risk of untreated depression to both mother and baby generally outweighs medication risks. Always discuss options with your OB-GYN or a perinatal psychiatrist.",null)
B.ahN=new A.fQ("Birth Plan Essentials","Labor & Delivery",7,'A birth plan is a written document that communicates your preferences for labor, delivery, and immediate postpartum care to your healthcare team. While labor rarely follows a script, having a birth plan helps ensure your values and wishes are respected and opens dialogue with your provider about what matters most to you. Research suggests that women who create birth plans feel more prepared, more in control, and report higher satisfaction with their birth experience\u2014regardless of whether the plan was fully followed.\n\nKey elements to consider include: pain management preferences (epidural, nitrous oxide, hydrotherapy, breathing techniques), labor environment (lighting, music, who can be present), monitoring preferences (continuous vs. intermittent fetal monitoring), mobility during labor, pushing positions, and preferences around interventions like episiotomy or assisted delivery. For the immediate postpartum period, consider skin-to-skin contact, cord clamping timing (delayed cord clamping is supported by ACOG for at least 30\u201360 seconds in uncomplicated births), and feeding intentions.\n\nKeep your birth plan concise\u2014one page is ideal\u2014and share it with your OB or midwife at a prenatal visit before your due date. Bring multiple copies to the hospital or birth center. Most importantly, build flexibility into your plan and discuss "what if" scenarios with your provider. Understanding the reasons behind potential interventions helps you make informed decisions in the moment, even when labor takes unexpected turns.',null)
B.ahP=new A.fQ("Breastfeeding Guide for New Mothers","Postpartum",6,"Breastfeeding provides unparalleled nutritional and immunological benefits for newborns. Breast milk contains antibodies\u2014particularly secretory IgA\u2014that protect against respiratory infections, gastrointestinal illness, and ear infections. The WHO and ACOG recommend exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months, followed by continued breastfeeding alongside complementary foods for at least 12 months (WHO recommends up to 2 years). Breastfeeding also benefits mothers by reducing the risk of postpartum hemorrhage, aiding uterine involution, and lowering long-term risk of breast and ovarian cancers.\n\nEstablishing a good latch is the cornerstone of successful breastfeeding. The baby should take a large portion of the areola\u2014not just the nipple\u2014into their mouth, with lips flanged outward. Common holds include the cradle hold, football hold (useful after C-section), and side-lying position for nighttime feeds. Newborns typically feed 8\u201312 times per 24 hours, and feeding on demand (cue-based feeding) supports milk supply regulation. Signs of effective feeding include audible swallowing, adequate wet diapers (6+ per day after day 4), and steady weight gain after the initial 5\u20137% loss in the first few days.\n\nCommon challenges include nipple soreness (often due to poor latch), engorgement, mastitis, and concerns about milk supply. Most issues can be addressed with the help of a certified lactation consultant (IBCLC). If breastfeeding is not possible or chosen, modern infant formula provides complete nutrition and feeding your baby in a way that works for your family is what matters most.",null)
B.ahK=new A.fQ("Iron & Prenatal Vitamins: What You Need","Nutrition",4,"Prenatal vitamins are a foundational supplement during pregnancy, designed to fill nutritional gaps that diet alone may not cover. The most critical nutrient is folate (or folic acid in synthetic form): 400\u2013800 mcg daily, ideally starting at least one month before conception, reduces the risk of neural tube defects like spina bifida by up to 70%. Women with a history of neural tube defect-affected pregnancies may need 4,000 mcg under medical supervision.\n\nIron is equally important\u2014pregnancy increases iron requirements to 27 mg/day due to expanded blood volume and fetal needs. Iron-deficiency anemia in pregnancy is associated with preterm birth, low birth weight, and postpartum depression. Ferrous sulfate is the most commonly recommended iron supplement, though ferrous gluconate and ferrous fumarate are alternatives for those with GI side effects. Consuming iron-rich foods (red meat, legumes, fortified cereals) alongside vitamin C enhances absorption, while calcium and coffee/tea can inhibit it\u2014so timing matters.\n\nOther key nutrients in prenatal vitamins include calcium (1,000 mg/day), vitamin D (600 IU/day minimum, though many providers recommend 1,000\u20132,000 IU), iodine (220 mcg/day for thyroid and fetal brain development), and DHA (an omega-3 fatty acid, 200\u2013300 mg/day for fetal neural and visual development). Not all prenatal vitamins contain DHA\u2014check the label and consider a separate DHA supplement if needed. Always choose a prenatal vitamin with your provider's guidance, as formulations vary widely.",null)
