B.cgA={Nausea:0,Fatigue:1,Headache:2,Cramps:3,"Baby Blues / Mood":4,"Breast Soreness":5,"Perineal Pain":6,"Night Sweats":7}
B.bAD=s(["Most common in the first trimester (weeks 6\u201312)","Often peaks in the morning but can occur any time","Typically triggered by strong smells, an empty stomach, or fatigue","Usually improves by week 14\u201316 for most people"],t.s)
B.nD=new A.Hp(1,"mild")
B.ciC=new A.nE(B.bAD,"Nausea during pregnancy \u2014 often called morning sickness \u2014 is caused by the rapid rise in hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) and estrogen levels. Although uncomfortable, mild-to-moderate nausea is a normal sign of a healthy, developing pregnancy. Eating small, frequent meals and staying hydrated can significantly reduce symptoms. If you are unable to keep any food or fluids down for more than 24 hours, contact your care provider \u2014 this may indicate Hyperemesis Gravidarum (HG), which is treatable.","Contact your provider if: vomiting is severe or you cannot keep fluids down, you notice signs of dehydration (dark urine, dizziness), or nausea begins after week 9 with no prior history.",!1)
B.bup=s(["Most intense in the first and third trimesters","Often worse in the afternoon and early evening","Linked to progesterone, increased blood volume, and disrupted sleep","Typically improves in the second trimester (weeks 13\u201328)"],t.s)
B.ciE=new A.nE(B.bup,"Pregnancy fatigue is one of the most common symptoms, driven by your body's extraordinary work: producing extra blood, building the placenta, and adjusting hormone levels. This is completely normal. Prioritising rest, light exercise (e.g. walking), staying hydrated, and eating iron-rich foods (lean meat, legumes, spinach) can help. Persistent, worsening fatigue \u2014 especially with shortness of breath, pale skin, or heart palpitations \u2014 may indicate anaemia, which affects up to 52% of pregnant women globally (WHO). Your provider can check with a simple blood test.","Speak to your provider if fatigue is severe and interfering with daily life, or if accompanied by breathlessness, chest pain, or very pale skin.",!1)
B.bJM=s(["Common in the first trimester due to hormonal shifts and increased blood volume","Dehydration, poor posture, eye strain, and low blood sugar are frequent triggers","Tension headaches are the most common type in pregnancy","May recur in the third trimester due to altered sleep and posture"],t.s)
B.qy=new A.Hp(2,"moderate")
B.ciB=new A.nE(B.bJM,'Mild-to-moderate headaches are common in early pregnancy, primarily due to surging hormone levels, increased blood circulation, and changes in blood pressure. Paracetamol (acetaminophen) at the lowest effective dose is considered safe for short-term use \u2014 avoid ibuprofen and aspirin unless directed by your provider. Staying well-hydrated, maintaining regular meal times, and gentle neck stretches are effective preventive measures. IMPORTANT: A sudden, severe "thunderclap" headache, or headache accompanied by visual disturbances, swelling of hands/face, or upper abdominal pain after 20 weeks may be a sign of pre-eclampsia \u2014 seek care immediately.',"Seek immediate care if: headache is sudden and severe, accompanied by visual changes (flashing lights, blurred vision), swelling of face or hands, or upper right abdominal pain \u2014 these can indicate pre-eclampsia.",!1)
B.bDK=s(["Round ligament pain (sharp, brief cramps) is normal from weeks 14\u201327","Braxton Hicks contractions (practice contractions) are common after week 20","Mild cramping similar to period pain is common in early pregnancy","Cramping after exercise or sexual activity is usually benign"],t.s)
B.ciD=new A.nE(B.bDK,"Mild, brief cramping during pregnancy is common and usually caused by the uterus expanding, round ligament stretching, or Braxton Hicks contractions. These are typically irregular, last only a few seconds, and ease with rest or position change. However, cramping can sometimes indicate something that needs attention: persistent cramping, cramping with bleeding, or regular tightening before 37 weeks (preterm labour) should always be evaluated by your care team. Staying hydrated, avoiding prolonged standing, and gentle stretching can help manage round ligament discomfort.","Contact your provider if: cramping is severe or worsening, accompanied by vaginal bleeding or unusual discharge, regular and rhythmic (may indicate preterm labour), or you are fewer than 37 weeks pregnant.",!1)
B.bAf=s(["Mood symptoms are recurring across the postpartum window rather than showing up as isolated difficult days","The baby blues are common in the first 1 to 2 weeks postpartum, but persistence beyond that window matters more clinically","Patterns of tearfulness, overwhelm, or anxiety are more important when they remain frequent over time"],t.s)
B.ciG=new A.nE(B.bAf,"Recurring postpartum mood symptoms can still be part of early recovery, but persistence, worsening intensity, or functional impact raises concern for postpartum depression or anxiety rather than transient baby blues alone.","Escalate if symptoms are worsening, interfering with sleep or bonding, or persisting beyond the first 2 weeks postpartum.",!1)
B.bxT=s(["Breast soreness is appearing repeatedly rather than just around one feeding transition","This pattern is more meaningful when it stays frequent after milk coming in or latch adjustment should be settling","Persistent soreness deserves closer attention if it is trending upward or paired with redness or fever"],t.s)
B.ciF=new A.nE(B.bxT,"Breast soreness is common postpartum, especially with engorgement and latch adjustment, but frequent ongoing soreness may signal unresolved feeding mechanics, engorgement, or evolving mastitis if inflammatory symptoms appear.","Escalate if soreness is worsening or associated with fever, redness, warmth, or flu-like symptoms.",!1)
B.bxQ=s(["Perineal pain is recurring across the recovery window instead of tapering quickly","This pattern matters more when soreness is not gradually easing over time","Persistent perineal pain can reflect slower healing even when it began as an expected postpartum symptom"],t.s)
B.ciA=new A.nE(B.bxQ,"Perineal pain is common early postpartum, especially after tearing or stitches, but a repeated or non-improving pattern suggests healing may be slower than expected and deserves monitoring.","Escalate if pain is worsening, foul-smelling, associated with fever, or not improving over time.",!1)
B.bv3=s(["Night sweats are happening on multiple days across the postpartum window","This can still fit normal postpartum hormone and fluid shifts when systemic illness signs are absent","The pattern becomes more clinically important if it is persistent, worsening, or paired with fever or chills"],t.s)
B.ciz=new A.nE(B.bv3,"Night sweats are common in postpartum recovery because hormone levels fall and extra fluid is shed, but repeated symptoms should still be interpreted in context with infection or thyroid warning signs.","Escalate if night sweats are accompanied by fever, chills, feeling unwell, or breast redness/pain suggestive of infection.",!1)
B.Ey=new A.an(B.cgA,[B.ciC,B.ciE,B.ciB,B.ciD,B.ciG,B.ciF,B.ciA,B.ciz],A.at("an<e,nE>"))
B.yF=new A.va(2,"down")
