B.ahl=new A.cG("Late postpartum follow-up with primary care or OB/GYN","Ongoing care",30,"Primary care clinic or OB/GYN office","Late postpartum still deserves structured follow-up through the first year, especially if recovery never fully settled, mood symptoms persist, or new health concerns have appeared over time.","Review menstrual changes, contraception, mood, pelvic floor function, exercise tolerance, sexual health, and longer-term health after pregnancy.","Bring up anything that has not normalized since birth, including mood, pelvic pain, bladder symptoms, sex-related pain, or persistent exhaustion.",null,B.aE,0)
B.cMm=new A.yR(B.ahl,"Primary care follow-up","Recommended",30,"Late postpartum care should feel like fully integrated ongoing care through the first year, not abandoned recovery.",!0,"If symptoms linger",21,"Pelvic floor symptoms that continue this late deserve evaluation rather than being normalized away.","If feeding changes are active",21,"Weaning, pumping, supply shifts, and breast pain can still need support later in the postpartum year.","Important if symptoms persist",14,"Late-onset or persistent postpartum depression, anxiety, OCD, and trauma symptoms still deserve prompt attention throughout the first postpartum year.")
B.ahC=new A.cG("Postpartum recovery follow-up with primary care or OB/GYN","Monthly or as needed",21,"Primary care clinic or OB/GYN office","Beyond the formal early postpartum window, many parents still need recovery-focused follow-up for pelvic floor symptoms, lactation, contraception, fatigue, thyroid issues, or mood changes.","Review ongoing recovery, fatigue, sexual health, contraception, exercise tolerance, breastfeeding changes, and late-emerging symptoms.","Bring up any symptoms that still linger, new pain, pelvic heaviness, thyroid-type symptoms, sexual health concerns, or fatigue that feels out of proportion.",null,B.aE,0)
B.cMn=new A.yR(B.ahC,"Primary care follow-up","Recommended this season",30,"By months 3 to 6 postpartum, primary care often becomes the main home for fatigue, thyroid, blood pressure, chronic disease, and general health concerns.",!0,"Useful now",21,"Pelvic floor symptoms, return-to-exercise goals, and core weakness often need attention in this stage.","If breastfeeding or pumping",21,"Feeding transitions, pumping, supply changes, and work-return logistics often become relevant during months 3 to 6.","Worth booking",14,"Postpartum mood disorders can still be active or late-emerging well after the standard 6-week visit.")
B.ahv=new A.cG("Early postpartum check-in with OB or midwife","Recommended soon",3,"OB or midwife clinic","ACOG recommends contact with a maternal provider within the first 3 weeks postpartum. In the earliest weeks, the focus is recovery, blood pressure, bleeding, pain, feeding, and emotional wellbeing.","Review bleeding, blood pressure, incision or perineal healing, pain control, feeding, sleep, and urgent warning signs.","Bring questions about bleeding, pain, incision or perineal healing, breastfeeding, sleep, mood, and when to call urgently.",null,B.aE,0)
B.cMo=new A.yR(B.ahv,"Primary care handoff planning","Not urgent yet",30,"The early weeks are mostly maternal-care-provider-led, but it is still helpful to plan the handoff to ongoing primary care.",!1,"Later in recovery",28,"Pelvic floor rehab usually becomes more relevant once early healing is more established.","Recommended soon",4,"The first 2 weeks are a high-value window for latch, supply, nipple pain, and pumping support.","Important to monitor",10,"Early postpartum emotional changes are common, but worsening anxiety, panic, or persistent low mood deserve closer review.")
B.ahq=new A.cG("Postpartum follow-up with OB or midwife","Recommended soon",7,"OB or midwife clinic","At about 4 weeks postpartum, ACOG-style care treats postpartum recovery as ongoing, not one single visit. If the full postpartum visit has not happened yet, this is the right time to book it and review bleeding, blood pressure, pain, mood, feeding, sleep, contraception, and recovery.","Review physical healing, mood and anxiety symptoms, feeding, sleep, contraception, blood pressure concerns, and return to exercise.","Ask about recovery progress, bleeding, pelvic floor symptoms, pain, sleep, mood changes, feeding challenges, contraception, and safe return to exercise.",null,B.aE,0)
B.cMp=new A.yR(B.ahq,"Primary care follow-up","Helpful next step",21,"Primary care follow-up should be identified as postpartum care transitions into long-term care.",!0,"Worth planning now",14,"Around the 6-week mark, pelvic floor rehab becomes increasingly useful for pressure, leakage, pain, and staged return to activity.","Still valuable",7,"Around weeks 3 to 6, lactation follow-up can help with pumping transitions, supply regulation, or persistent breast pain.","Important this month",10,"Mood and anxiety symptoms that continue or worsen beyond the initial baby-blues window deserve follow-up rather than waiting longer.")
B.ahi=new A.cG("Postpartum follow-up with OB or midwife","Recommended soon",7,"OB or midwife clinic","Once the birth date is set, Contessa can tailor timing more precisely. The default postpartum recommendation is an early follow-up with a maternal care provider plus a comprehensive postpartum visit by 12 weeks.","Review bleeding, blood pressure, pain, mood, feeding, sleep, and the plan for ongoing recovery care.","Ask about recovery progress, mood, feeding, sleep, pain, and the timing of your next postpartum visit.",null,B.aE,0)
B.cMq=new A.yR(B.ahi,"Primary care follow-up","Helpful next step",21,"Postpartum care should transition into ongoing primary care, especially if there were general health concerns during pregnancy or after birth.",!0,"Useful once cleared",21,"Pelvic floor rehab is often helpful once early healing is underway.","Useful if feeding is active",7,"Lactation support can help early feeding, pain, pumping, or supply questions.","Important to monitor",14,"Emotional wellbeing deserves follow-up throughout the postpartum year, not just at one single visit.")
B.ahg=new A.cG("Comprehensive postpartum visit with OB, midwife, or maternity provider","Important this month",14,"OB or midwife clinic","The comprehensive postpartum visit should be completed by 12 weeks postpartum and should address physical, social, and psychological wellbeing.","Discuss contraception, pelvic floor recovery, mood, chronic condition follow-up, feeding goals, and safe return to exercise or sex.","Review mood, contraception, sex, exercise, pelvic floor function, sleep, feeding goals, and any symptoms still not improving.",null,B.aE,0)
B.cMr=new A.yR(B.ahg,"Primary care follow-up","Important this month",21,"This is a good time to solidify the transition from maternity care to primary care for ongoing health maintenance.",!0,"Useful now",14,"By this stage, pelvic floor rehab is often appropriate for postpartum rebuilding and symptom recovery.","If feeding is ongoing",14,"Lactation support remains useful for return-to-work pumping plans, pain, weaning decisions, or supply concerns.","Worth booking",14,"Postpartum depression, anxiety, OCD, or trauma symptoms can emerge or intensify later in the first 3 months.")
